10-I11), which could have some positive outcomes for future White moves.\n\nI considered a “G8” move to block Row 8. Playing this could block several possible threats by Black. I decided this wasn\'t the right choice. Black could then extend, forming four. Playing I11 or E7 would stop this threat.\n\nI reviewed my conclusions: I concluded that White must block at either E7 or I11.\n\nI weighed the two options of E7 or I11 for a long time. They are the best defensive choices.\n\nI analyzed if I could block at I11 or E7. Blocking at E7 or I11 would both block Black\'s immediate threat. I considered how this would affect me. I also checked potential follow-up moves, evaluating if the move would result in a win.\n\nFinally, I decided on "I11" because it\'s slightly better for White’s shape on the board.\n\nI11 would connect with my other pieces, whereas E7 is isolated. I double-checked the coordinates.\n\nThis results in the following move: {"col": "I", "row": 11}.\n\n\n', 'output': 'Evaluation: The current board shows a critical threat from Black. Black has formed a diagonal "Open 3" with stones at F8, G9, and H10. If Black is allowed to play at I11 or E7, they will form an "Open 4" (or a 4-in-a-row that guarantees a 5 on the next move). Specifically, if Black plays I11, they threaten to win with E7 or J12. If White blocks J12, Black plays E7 to connect 5. Thus, White must block this diagonal immediately.\n\nThere are two blocking candidates: E7 and I11.\n1.  **Blocking at E7:** This stops the diagonal from the top. However, the stone at E7 would be isolated and offers little connection to other White stones.\n2.  **Blocking at I11:** This stops the diagonal from the bottom. Importantly, playing at I11 connects with White\'s existing stones at I9 and I10, forming a vertical line of three stones (I9-I10-I11). Although this vertical line is blocked by Black\'s I8, forming a connected group is generally superior to placing isolated stones for future potential.\n\nTherefore, I11 is the best defensive move that also improves White\'s board shape.\n\nReason: Black has a lethal Open 3 (F8-G9-H10). White must block at I11 or E7 to prevent Black from forming a 4-in-a-row and winning. I11 is the better choice as it connects with I9 and I10 to form a defensive cluster, whereas E7 leaves the stone isolated.\n\nAnswer: {"col": "I", "row": 11}'}
